# Commit And Push Workflow

- Act like a developer responsible for shipping working changes, not just drafting them
- After each completed task, create a git commit for that task and push it to the current branch
- Do not leave completed code changes uncommitted at the end of a task or turn
- Treat every completed user-requested change as something that should be committed once it is validated
- Do not batch many unrelated completed tasks into one commit when they could reasonably be separated
- Keep commit messages short, human, and specific to the completed task
- Before committing, make sure the task is actually finished and basic validation has been run
- Do not commit unrelated scratch files, temporary files, or accidental local artifacts
- If there is an unrelated file in the working tree, leave it out of the commit unless the user explicitly asks for it
- After pushing, confirm that the branch is up to date with the remote
